d to be shorter than dildos and have a flared base that prevents them from being lost in the rectum."},"skos:broader":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001266"},"skos:related":[{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000043"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000081"}],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001266"},"dc:replaces":{"@id":"http://homosaurus.org/v2/buttplugs"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2019-05-14"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2024-02-01"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"}},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0003124","dc:identifier":"homoit0003124","skos:prefLabel":{"@language":"en","@value":"Douching"},"rdfs:comment":{"@language":"en","@value":"The use of a device (called a douche or enema) to introduce a stream of liquid into either the rectum or vagina. Can be used for sexual or erotic purposes."},"skos:broader":[{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000442"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001267"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001299"}],"skos:related":[{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000043"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000076"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000081"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000293"}],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000404"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-12-20"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2024-05-15"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"}},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000044","dc:identifier":"homoit0000044","skos:prefLabel":{"@language":"en","@value":"Androgen insensitivity syndrome"},"skos:altLabel":[{"@language":"en","@value":"AIS"},{"@language":"en","@value":"Goldberg-Maxwell syndrome"},{"@language":"en","@value":"Male androgen insensitivity syndrome"}],"rdfs:comment":{"@language":"en","@value":"People born with AIS have XY chromosomes and testes, and their bodies have varying levels of insensitivity to androgens (including testosterone). This means that their cells do not respond typically to the testosterone that they produce. Like other variations in sex characteristics, AIS is a spectrum, with the subcategories of complete (CAIS), partial (PAIS), and mild androgen insensitivity (MAIS). The term \"male androgen insensitivity syndrome\" should not be used to describe this variation because many people with AIS are women."},"skos:broader":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669"},"skos:narrower":[{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001079"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002830"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002866"}],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269"},"dc:replaces":{"@id":"http://homosaurus.org/v2/androgenInsensitivitySyndrome"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2019-05-14"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2024-01-30"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"}},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001079","dc:identifier":"homoit0001079","skos:prefLabel":{"@language":"en","@value":"Partial androgen insensitivity syndrome"},"skos:altLabel":{"@language":"en","@value":"PAIS"},"rdfs:comment":{"@language":"en","@value":"People with partial androgen insensitivity (PAIS) have some response to testosterone, but not as much as is typical for people with XY chromosomes without androgen insensitivity. People with PAIS may be born with testes that are undescended or partially descended and with genital differences such as a vaginal opening that is shallower than typical, a phallus that may be perceived as a large clitoris or a small penis, and variations affecting their urethra. They might also be born with genitals that look more like a penis or like a vulva and vagina. In adolescence, people with PAIS may develop some features that are associated with a typical testosterone puberty and others that are associated with a typical estrogen puberty since their body responds to some of the testosterone that their gonads produce while the rest is converted into estrogen."},"skos:broader":[{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000044"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669"}],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269"},"dc:replaces":{"@id":"http://homosaurus.org/v2/partialAndrogenInsensitivitySyndrome"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2019-05-14"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-30"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"}},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002830","dc:identifier":"homoit0002830","skos:prefLabel":{"@language":"en","@value":"Complete gonadal dysgenesis"},"skos:altLabel":{"@language":"en","@value":"Pure gonadal dysgenesis"},"rdfs:comment":{"@language":"en","@value":"People with complete gonadal dysgenesis are born with XX or XY chromosomes and gonadal tissue that has not developed into testicles or ovaries (also known as streak gonads). They are generally born with a vulva and vagina, and they will not usually develop secondary sex characteristics at puberty because streak gonads do not produce hormones."},"skos:broader":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000044"},"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-09-14"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"}},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002866","dc:identifier":"homoit0002866","skos:prefLabel":{"@language":"en","@value":"Mild androgen insensitivity syndrome"},"skos:altLabel":{"@language":"en","@value":"MAIS"},"rdfs:comment":{"@language":"en","@value":"People with mild androgen insensitivity syndrome (MAIS) respond to testosterone at a somewhat reduced level compared to typical levels, which means that they will usually be born with a penis (which may or may not be smaller than typical) and will later develop secondary sex characteristics associated with a typical testosterone puberty. Because their bodies convert a small amount of their naturally produced androgens into estrogen, they may have visible variations in features, such as the amount of facial hair, body hair, or muscle tone they develop. They may also experience some breast development during puberty."},"skos:broader":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000044"},"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001269"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-28"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-09-14"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"}},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000045","dc:identifier":"homoit0000045","skos:prefLabel":{"@language":"en","@value":"Androgynous people"},"rdfs:comment":{"@language":"en","@value":"People who have a non-binary gender that includes masculine and feminine traits."},"skos:broader":[{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001378"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001817"}],"skos:related":[{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000047"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001048"}],"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000915"},"dc:replaces":{"@id":"http://homosaurus.org/v2/androgynousPeople"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2019-05-14"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2022-12-13"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"}},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000047","dc:identifier":"homoit0000047","skos:prefLabel":{"@language":"en","@value":"Androgyny"},"rdfs:comment":{"@language":"en","@value":"The combination of masculine and feminine characteristics."},"skos:broader":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000568"},"skos:related":[{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000045"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000389"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000562"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000577"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000995"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001756"}],"skos:closeMatch":{"@id":"http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85004920"},"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000560"},"dc:replaces":{"@id":"http://homosaurus.org/v2/androgyny"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2019-05-14"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2023-06-27"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"}},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000046","dc:identifier":"homoit0000046","skos:prefLabel":{"@language":"he","@value":"Androgynos"},"rdfs:comment":{"@language":"en","@value":"Israeli term used in Rabbinical literature to describe both an intersex mythological creature and refer to a legal condition."},"skos:broader":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000322"},"skos:related":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000618"},"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000560"},"dc:replaces":{"@id":"http://homosaurus.org/v2/androgynos"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2019-05-14"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2024-01-31"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"}},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000618","dc:identifier":"homoit0000618","skos:prefLabel":{"@language":"en","@value":"Intersex people"},"skos:altLabel":[{"@language":"en","@value":"Hermaphrodites"},{"@language":"en","@value":"Intersexuals"},{"@language":"en","@value":"Hermaphroditic people"},{"@language":"en","@value":"Intersex-identified people"},{"@language":"en","@value":"Intersexual people"}],"rdfs:comment":{"@language":"en","@value":"Individuals born with variations in their genitalia, hormones, internal anatomy, and/or chromosomes that do not fully or exclusively conform to medical or social expectations for males or females. Only use to describe people who describe themselves as intersex."},"skos:broader":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000915"},"skos:narrower":[{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000668"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000674"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002825"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0003039"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0003225"}],"skos:related":[{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000046"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000352"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000669"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000670"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000671"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000672"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000673"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001759"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001763"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001767"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0001822"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0002744"}],"skos:exactMatch":{"@id":"http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh2007003860"},"skos:hasTopConcept":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000915"},"dc:replaces":{"@id":"http://homosaurus.org/v2/intersexPeople"},"dc:issued":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2019-05-14"},"dc:modified":{"@type":"xsd:date","@value":"2024-08-01"},"@type":"skos:Concept","skos:inScheme":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3"}},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000568","dc:identifier":"homoit0000568","skos:prefLabel":{"@language":"en","@value":"Gender expression"},"rdfs:comment":{"@language":"en","@value":"Term used to describe the way individuals express their gender identity through their appearance and behaviors, including gestures, movement, dress, and grooming."},"skos:broader":{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000560"},"skos:narrower":[{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/v3/homoit0000047"},{"@id":"https://homosaurus.org/